A||N||19||GT-maniac||Moving battery to trunk||||1||
Z||000000||GT-maniac||05-19-2001||08:07 PM||||I'm thinking of moving my battery into the trunk area. I saw a pic of this done on a highly modified fiero at some show and it seemed like a good idea to me. The battery was sitting in a tray which partially sat on that little shelf on the right side of the trunk. Has anyone here done this mod ? It would keep it out of the rain and would be a whole lot easier and cheaper than moving the battery to the front. Just have to extend the cables a bit and cut a hole through the trunk wall. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||mbramble||05-19-2001||08:25 PM||||My 85 GT project car had the battery in the trunk when I bought it.  Also had a "sharing unit" or whatever it is called that allows you to run two batteries.  Don't have a clue why.  He also had mounted a fan in the bottom of the trunk to blow air out -- to get rid of battery gasses, I guess. Anyway, there is precious little room in the trunk already so I moved it up front.  I built my own front mounted tray from photos and dimensions of the ones you can buy for a grand total of about $10 for fiberglass cloth and resin.  I had some #2 cable around so I used that to extend the battery cable up to the front.  I ran it thru Schedule 80 electical conduit clamped to the coolant lines under the car.  The only thing that I bought was a new set of cables for the battery -- the negative one is bolted to the frame for ground and the positive one was connected to the cable coming from the back of the car.<P>Disadvantges to putting it in the back would be that it does take up that space  in the trunk and it also moves that weight even further back than it already is.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||05-19-2001||08:57 PM||||Interesting thread already.<BR>Sooner or later I'll move my battery to the trunk for a "handling and traction enhancement".<BR>Like to see some pictures of this.<BR>Can't fathom why anybody would put the battery in the front compartment unless they like more understeer and heavier steering.<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||05-19-2001||09:00 PM||||Only thing that worries me is getting the wires back to the engine compartment.  Its a little dangerous to leave them exposed like that, has anyone tried to cover them or anything?||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||05-19-2001||09:51 PM||||I would move it to the front. I would never move it to the trunk. Why, I dont get it. Keep it out of the rain. Mine has currently been there for 178xxx miles and is just fine. If you do move it to the trunk better make some holes for vent air. And a drain,||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||05-19-2001||10:25 PM||||move it to the front - no where else - period||||reg||1
Z||000006||Vlad||05-19-2001||10:58 PM||||Let me disagree. Four months ago I put the battery in the trank (on the right shelf). <BR>I have no problems whatsoever with gases, weight distribution or any other stuff.<BR>I used Pontiac Firefly OEM plastic tray which bolted to the shelf. Now, the problem is that it's difficult to get to another side to bolt the tray. Well, impossible without taking bamper cover upart. <BR>So I mounted the tray with 4 ****  metal screws. Since there is no weight pressure on the tray, but on the shelf (read frame), all contraction is solid. It has proved itself in several autocrossing events.<BR>Tha best part of this mod is its price. 5 dollars for the tray off nearest junkyard. <BR>Some extention to the positive cable and you can bolt negative to the frame in the trank.<BR>I kept it in original location though...<BR>Yeah... One hole in the trank wall to run cables. 40 minuts of work. <BR>I'm happy Fiero driver.......)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||05-19-2001||11:03 PM||||Oops I misunderstood (I've never even heard of someone putting it in the trunk I thought he meant the front compartment).  Why would you want it in the trunk?  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Vlad||05-19-2001||11:20 PM||||Why not......?<P>I didn't want it to be in its original place because it was all rusted out. Now, while puting the battery in the front is a good and healthy idea, implementation  involved too much time and money. I have to work on my car in condo underground parking so it makes some limitations to what I can do.<BR>Since I needed to move the battery anyway, I put it in the trunk. And I'm not sorry....||||reg||1
Z||000009||GT-maniac||05-20-2001||12:38 AM||||Some interesting replies so far.. I for one can't see why everyone seems so determined to move the battery to the front. As gixxer said there's enough weight there already and it's not like batteries are enormously heavy anyway. I also don't like the idea of running long cables underneath the car, even with conduit protecting them. As Vlad says it seems like a lot of extra effort and cost for no real gain that I can see. <BR>And for the guy that wondered why bother too move the battery at all, I guess it doesn't rain too much where you are. I've found out from experience that having to dry off the battery terminals just to get the car started is a real pain. Especially as it's usually raining at the time, or even better snowing.<BR>Vlad should know as he lives in Vancouver which is pretty much the rain capital of Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Vlad||05-20-2001||02:21 AM||||&gt;&gt;Vancouver which is pretty much the rain &gt;&gt;&gt;capital of Canada.<P>THIS IS NOT TRUE.........)))||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fierowrecker||05-20-2001||07:37 AM||||Hey GT!<BR>You misunderstand the Fiero weight balance...<BR>More weight is in the rear of the Fiero than in the front, this causes the understeer...<BR>Moving the batery helps eliminate the problem, and adding a rear sway bar really improves the handling!<BR>I also have 100 lbs. of lead shot in the spare tire well to help balance the car...<BR>When I want to slide my Fiero around a corner, the nose and rear slide equally...<P><BR>[QUOTE]Originally posted by GT-maniac:<BR>[B]Some interesting replies so far.. I for one can't see why everyone seems so determined to move the battery to the front. As gixxer said there's enough weight there already and it's not like batteries are enormously heavy anyway. I also don't like the idea of running long cables underneath the car, even with conduit protecting them. As Vlad says it seems like a lot of extra effort and cost for no real gain that I can see. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||05-20-2001||09:43 AM||||for all the rationalization going on here for moving the battery to the rear trunk, sounds like the REAL reason he wants to do it<P>is cause the battery tray is rusted out.<P>that is usually caused by a bad altinator overcharging the battery, or it was overfilled, and leaked acid out, not from rain.<P>   do you really want the same thing to happen in your trunk? Moving it back there cause the tray is rusted out would be trashy.<P> also the negative terminal is grounded to the engine block, not to the frame, and if you drill a hole through the trunk make certain you get one of those rubber grommet things to protect the + wire, otherwise its only a matter of time till the metal cuts through, shorts the battery to the frame, and you have an unscheduled Fiero roast.||||reg||1
Z||000013||GT-maniac||05-20-2001||11:47 AM||||Actually the battery tray is not rusted out at all. But hey if you want to believe that it is, that's your prerogrative. And Vlad I lived in Victoria for over 30 years, if it rains more somewhere else in Canada I'd sure like to know where it is. In Victoria it rains pretty much every day from sept to may. That's one of the reasons why I left as I was tired of the endless rain.<BR>Fierowrecker you're right I hadn't really considered the fiero's weight balance that way, I like the idea of moving some weight to the front if as you say it will improve handling.||||reg||1
Z||000014||gixxer||05-20-2001||12:00 PM||||Fierowrecker, everything being equal, if the car is already perfectly balanced, moving weight to the front will make it understeer.<BR>The reasons for this are in the nature of the contruction and flexibility of the tire's carcass.<BR>To fix the understeer, you would put a (larger) anti-roll bar on the back. One of the purposes of an anti-roll bar is to balance the car by diminishing traction at that end of the car by loading up the outside tire in a corner. It would also remove weight from the inside tire on that end of the car. This tire pair has less traction.<BR>Now you have less overall traction front and rear.<BR>It's generally accepted that for equal size tires, a 50/50 weight distribution is best for steady state cornering.But, for overall performance, a slight tail heavy condition is best.<BR>And if you have larger rear tires, you can go ahead and move the CG farther back still, while still keeping the car balanced in corners and benefit from increased traction exiting corners (and whupping on Mustangs).<BR>But don't take my word for it, read some books on the subject.<BR>The Fiero has a weight distribution that a Camaro or Mustang owner would die for (for traction). Yet for the most part, Fiero owners seem to think a small rearward weight bias is something evil and should be "fixed".<BR>Maybe you guys should should tell the CART and Formula 1 guys how they are doing it all wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <BR>My battery will probably end up going the trunk, but I'm in no hurry as nothing is rusted out yet.<BR>I'll get off the soapbox now.     ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Tina||05-20-2001||12:51 PM||||Got mine in the trunk now for the last year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Sitting in aftermarket batterybox available from most part stores.(Comes with lid)  <BR>No problems whatsoever, just like earlier mentioned pay attention to wires going in to enginebay.<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jaei Sun||05-20-2001||01:43 PM||||&lt;sarcastic tone&gt; Why would anyone want to move the battery to the front??<P> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/battery.jpg">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||GT-maniac||05-20-2001||04:15 PM||||Thanks Tina, that's what I was after, someone with actual experience doing this. Not sarcastic comments from the battery must go in the front or else crowd. I'd already read the thread on moving the battery to the front and didn't really want to start another long flame war about it. Thanks to all who replied with constructive comments and to all those who must have the battery in front I'm happy that that extra two percent front weight bias was so important for you. But your way is not necessarily the only way things can be done.||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||05-20-2001||04:57 PM||||<B>I've found out from experience that having to dry off the battery terminals just to get the car started is a real pain.</B><P>What the hell are you talking about there?! A wet battery makes for no start? Maybe everything flip-flops on the border but I've never had to dry off my battery terminals to start any of my cars.<P><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||cadaver||05-20-2001||10:45 PM||||I was just going to ask the same thing. ||||reg||1
